# Shell scripts must use LF line endings. CRLF breaks heredoc delimiters and
# case/if matches in bash on Linux/macOS, and adds stray \r to variable values
# on Git Bash. Force LF in the working tree on all platforms (overrides
# core.autocrlf) so CI and local runs execute identical bytes.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Go source files: CRLF breaks gofmt checks on CI (Linux checks out LF, but
# Windows autocrlf may leave CRLF in the working tree). Force LF everywhere.
*.go text eol=lf

# YAML config files (CI workflows, compose, etc.): CRLF can break heredoc and
# here-anchor parsing in some YAML loaders. Force LF for consistency.
*.yml text eol=lf

# JSON files (config, lockfiles): CRLF is technically valid JSON but causes
# noisy diffs and can break checksums in content-addressed tooling. Force LF.
*.json text eol=lf

# Frontend source files: TS/TSX/CSS/MJS. CRLF breaks some tooling (esbuild
# source maps, eslint parsers) and creates noisy cross-platform diffs. Force LF
# so CI (Linux) and local (Windows autocrlf) stay byte-identical.
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
